  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1033 PM CDT MON AUG 10 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening reveals upper level ridging\nbeginning to take shape across the southeast. An upper level\ntrough can be seen well to our north, which is helping guide an\narea of convection just south of the Great Lakes. The weak ridge\naxis in place across the south will slowly shift west over the\nnext several days, eventually expanding and strengthening late\nthis week and into the weekend. This will allow for temps to\nsteadily warm up through the week with highs eventually settling\ninto the mid to upper 90s for most of Central Alabama. Continued\nhumid conditions combined with these temps will bump heat indices\ninto the 105-110 degree range. Heat Advisories will be likely\ndaily starting Wednesday. The latest ECMWF EFI hints at unusually\nwarm temperatures for us over the weekend, generally 10 degrees\nabove normal for mid August. This will lead to a major heat risk\nfor Central Alabama late this week and over the weekend.\n\nThis pattern will promote a typically summertime convection\nregime over the next couple of days with low to moderate (20-40%)\nchances for showers and storms through Wednesday. Beyond\nWednesday, we will see reduced rain chances as the upper ridge\ntakes control and slightly drier air spread across the region.\n\nOtherwise, patchy fog is expected early Tuesday morning as dew\npoint depressions narrow and condensation pressure deficits call\nto 2-5mb. Locations that received rain earlier this afternoon and\nevening will be most likely to see reduced visibilities out the\ndoor Tuesday morning.\n\n95/Castillo",
